```sql
SELECT title, author, views, SCORE() AS relevance
FROM   "ch2_articles"
WHERE  MATCH(body, 'caching scaling')
ORDER BY relevance DESC;
-- Top results include Elasticsearch at Scale and Redis Caching Strategies

SELECT title, author, views
FROM   "ch2_articles"
WHERE  MATCH(body, 'database')
  AND  views > 800
ORDER BY views DESC;
-- Graph Databases Explained | Dan Lee | 850

SELECT author, COUNT(*) AS articles, SUM(views) AS total_views
FROM   "ch2_articles"
WHERE  tags = 'sql'
GROUP BY author
ORDER BY total_views DESC;
-- Alice Chen: 2 articles, 1670 views
```

SCORE() and MATCH() are Elasticsearch-specific — standard SQL has no equivalent. Array fields are flattened, so `WHERE tags = 'sql'` matches any document whose tags array contains `'sql'`. Elasticsearch SQL is a read-only subset: no JOINs, subqueries, window functions, or DML. For richer analytics use the Aggregations API; for general relational analytics use PostgreSQL.

