A social network is stored as a property graph in OrientDB: Person vertices connected by FOLLOWS edges. Variable-depth traversal and shortest-path are idiomatic in a graph database but require recursive CTEs in SQL.

Prerequisites: Docker with the daemon running, OrientDB 3.2+ reachable at `localhost:2424` (binary) and `http://localhost:2480` (HTTP/Studio), plus DBeaver with the OrientDB JDBC driver. No local Java or OrientDB tarball install is required for the Docker path.

```sql
-- OrientDB SQL — run in DBeaver (Driver: OrientDB) against demodb (Docker: localhost:2424, root/root).
-- On a fresh empty DB you can skip the DROP CLASS lines; IF EXISTS avoids errors on first run.

DROP CLASS FOLLOWS IF EXISTS UNSAFE;
DROP CLASS Person   IF EXISTS UNSAFE;

CREATE CLASS Person  EXTENDS V;
CREATE CLASS FOLLOWS EXTENDS E;

CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Alice', city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Bob',   city='London';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Carol', city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Dan',   city='Amsterdam';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Eve',   city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Frank', city='London';

CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Alice') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Bob');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Alice') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Carol');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Bob')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Carol') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Eve');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Eve')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Frank');

-- Graph: Alice → Bob → Dan → Eve → Frank
--        Alice → Carol → Dan

-- Part A stub: traverse FOLLOWS outward from Alice up to depth 2
SELECT $depth AS depth, name, city
FROM (
  TRAVERSE out('FOLLOWS')
  FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Alice')
  WHILE FALSE  -- implement: $depth <= 2
)
WHERE TRUE  -- implement: name <> 'Alice'
ORDER BY $depth, name;

-- Part B stub: shortest directed path Alice → Frank along FOLLOWS
SELECT shortestPath(
  (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Alice'),
  (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Frank'),
  'OUT',   -- keep OUT direction
  NULL     -- implement: edge class 'FOLLOWS'
).name AS path
FROM Person
LIMIT 1;
```

Tasks:
1. Complete the TRAVERSE stub so it follows `FOLLOWS` edges outward from Alice while `$depth <= 2`, excluding Alice herself. Confirm depth 1 is Bob and Carol, and depth 2 is Dan only.
2. Complete the `shortestPath()` stub so it finds the minimum-hop route from Alice to Frank along outgoing `FOLLOWS` edges. Confirm the path is `['Alice','Bob','Dan','Eve','Frank']`.

practice.answer:
Full Person/FOLLOWS seed plus TRAVERSE to depth 2 and shortestPath along FOLLOWS. Depth 1: Bob and Carol; depth 2: Dan once; path Alice→Bob→Dan→Eve→Frank (4 hops).

```sql
-- OrientDB SQL — run in DBeaver (Driver: OrientDB) against demodb (Docker: localhost:2424, root/root).
-- On a fresh empty DB you can skip the DROP CLASS lines; IF EXISTS avoids errors on first run.

DROP CLASS FOLLOWS IF EXISTS UNSAFE;
DROP CLASS Person   IF EXISTS UNSAFE;

CREATE CLASS Person  EXTENDS V;
CREATE CLASS FOLLOWS EXTENDS E;

CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Alice', city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Bob',   city='London';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Carol', city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Dan',   city='Amsterdam';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Eve',   city='Berlin';
CREATE VERTEX Person SET name='Frank', city='London';

CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Alice') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Bob');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Alice') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Carol');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Bob')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Carol') TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Dan')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Eve');
CREATE EDGE FOLLOWS FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Eve')   TO (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name='Frank');

SELECT $depth AS depth, name, city
FROM (
  TRAVERSE out('FOLLOWS')
  FROM (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Alice')
  WHILE $depth <= 2
)
WHERE name <> 'Alice'
ORDER BY $depth, name;
-- depth 1: Bob (London), Carol (Berlin)
-- depth 2: Dan (Amsterdam)

SELECT shortestPath(
  (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Alice'),
  (SELECT FROM Person WHERE name = 'Frank'),
  'OUT',
  'FOLLOWS'
).name AS path
FROM Person
LIMIT 1;
-- path: ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Dan', 'Eve', 'Frank']  (4 hops)
```

TRAVERSE exposes `$depth` with no explicit base-case / recursive-step syntax. `shortestPath()` is a first-class function (Dijkstra internally). The PostgreSQL equivalents need recursive CTEs that accumulate paths and guard cycles — much longer and easier to get wrong. Graph databases are built around traversal, reachability, and shortest-path.
